        "Background": "#FFB9F9E1"
      },
      {
        "Id": "e563cb9158f14bbc8e646f8ef1020151",
        "Title": "HOW TO USE:  open the floor plan where the walls go.  Input 2 = AUTO: the scale is decided and checked BEFORE anything is placed - DIMENSIONS WRITTEN ON THE PLAN are read first and give the scale; no dimension -> the thickest walls are taken as standard 200 / 150 mm walls; no scale that can be trusted -> nothing is placed and the result says why.  Grid lines with bubbles become Revit grids.  Dimensions (walls on a dimension point are set exactly at the written distance); text too small to read -> the result marks one dimension D1 on the plan picture: put what it says in input 2 as DIM 8850 and run again.  No dimensions -> SCALE (input 2): WALL 230 = the outer (thickest) walls are 230 mm thick; 1:100 = PDF drawn at 1:100; LINE 2000 = a selected detail line drawn on the picture is 2000 mm; WIDTH 12000 = building width.  0 = plan picture / PDF.  2 = scale: no dimensions on the plan -> WALL 230 (thickness of the outer walls);  PDF at 1:100 -> 1:100;  plan with a dimension or scale bar -> run once, draw a detail line over that dimension on the picture, select it, 2 = LINE 2000 (its real length), run again.  11 = False first to see what it finds (pop-up + marked picture).  It builds walls (thickness per wall), doors / windows in the gaps, the plan under them at true scale, and a 3D view.  Missing walls: draw them on the picture.",
        "DescriptionText": "",
        "IsExpanded": true,
        "WidthAdjustment": 0.0,
        "HeightAdjustment": 0.0,
        "Nodes": [],
        "HasNestedGroups": false,
        "Left": 0.0,
        "Top": -420.0,
        "Width": 1600.0,
